# Phiên âm + từ loại + nghĩa tiếng Anh ngắn cho 303 thẻ termbase sẵn có.
# Định dạng:  id | ipa | pos | senseEn
# pos: n = noun, v = verb, adj = adjective, adv = adverb, phr = phrase, abbr = abbreviation
# IPA theo giọng Mỹ (General American). Cụm nhiều từ ghi phiên âm cả cụm.

## artifact
product-backlog | /ˈprɑːdʌkt ˈbæklɑːɡ/ | n | the ordered list of everything that might be needed in the product
sprint-backlog | /sprɪnt ˈbæklɑːɡ/ | n | the work a team selects for one sprint, plus its plan for delivering it
epic | /ˈepɪk/ | n | a large body of work that is broken down into smaller stories
theme | /θiːm/ | n | a group of related work items sharing a common goal
kanban-board | /ˈkɑːnbɑːn bɔːrd/ | n | a visual board showing work items moving through workflow columns

## cadence
sprint | /sprɪnt/ | n | a short, fixed-length period in which a team delivers a usable increment
iteration | /ˌɪtəˈreɪʃn/ | n | one repeated cycle of building, reviewing and improving the product
release-planning | /rɪˈliːs ˈplænɪŋ/ | n | planning which features go into which upcoming release
cadence | /ˈkeɪdns/ | n | the steady, repeating rhythm at which events or deliveries happen

## communication
information-radiator | /ˌɪnfərˈmeɪʃn ˈreɪdieɪtər/ | n | a large visible display that broadcasts progress to anyone passing by
osmotic-communication | /ɑːzˈmɑːtɪk kəˌmjuːnɪˈkeɪʃn/ | n | information absorbed passively by overhearing others nearby
active-listening | /ˈæktɪv ˈlɪsnɪŋ/ | n | listening with full attention and reflecting back what was said
communication-channels | /kəˌmjuːnɪˈkeɪʃn ˈtʃænlz/ | n | the distinct paths along which information flows between people
stakeholder-engagement | /ˈsteɪkhoʊldər ɪnˈɡeɪdʒmənt/ | n | actively involving affected parties throughout the work
stakeholder-analysis | /ˈsteɪkhoʊldər əˈnæləsɪs/ | n | identifying stakeholders and assessing their interest and influence
knowledge-sharing | /ˈnɑːlɪdʒ ˈʃerɪŋ/ | n | deliberately spreading what people know across the team
tacit-knowledge | /ˈtæsɪt ˈnɑːlɪdʒ/ | n | know-how held in people's heads that is hard to write down
explicit-knowledge | /ɪkˈsplɪsɪt ˈnɑːlɪdʒ/ | n | knowledge that has been written down and can be passed on directly

## delivery
increment | /ˈɪŋkrəmənt/ | n | a working, usable piece of product added in one cycle
impediment | /ɪmˈpedɪmənt/ | n | anything blocking or slowing the team's progress

## engineering
pair-programming | /per ˈproʊɡræmɪŋ/ | n | two developers working together at one workstation
tdd | /ˌtest ˈdrɪvn dɪˈveləpmənt/ | n | writing the test before writing the code that makes it pass
atdd | /əkˈseptəns ˌtest ˈdrɪvn dɪˈveləpmənt/ | n | defining acceptance tests with the customer before development starts
bdd | /bɪˈheɪvjər ˌdrɪvn dɪˈveləpmənt/ | n | describing behaviour in plain language that doubles as an automated test
continuous-integration | /kənˈtɪnjuəs ˌɪntɪˈɡreɪʃn/ | n | merging and automatically testing everyone's code many times a day
refactoring | /riːˈfæktərɪŋ/ | n | improving code structure without changing what it does
technical-debt | /ˈteknɪkl det/ | n | the future cost of choosing a quick solution over a clean one
continuous-delivery | /kənˈtɪnjuəs dɪˈlɪvəri/ | n | keeping software always in a state where it can be released
feature-toggle | /ˈfiːtʃər ˈtɑːɡl/ | n | a switch that turns a feature on or off without redeploying
collective-code-ownership | /kəˈlektɪv koʊd ˈoʊnərʃɪp/ | n | any team member may change any part of the codebase
coding-standards | /ˈkoʊdɪŋ ˈstændərdz/ | n | agreed rules for how code should be written and formatted
devops | /ˈdevɑːps/ | n | merging development and operations so releases are frequent and reliable
mob-programming | /mɑːb ˈproʊɡræmɪŋ/ | n | the whole team working on one item at one computer
unit-testing | /ˈjuːnɪt ˈtestɪŋ/ | n | automated tests that check one small piece of code in isolation
test-automation | /test ˌɔːtəˈmeɪʃn/ | n | running tests by script instead of by hand

## estimation
story-point | /ˈstɔːri pɔɪnt/ | n | a relative unit expressing how big a piece of work is
planning-poker | /ˈplænɪŋ ˈpoʊkər/ | n | estimating by revealing cards simultaneously, then discussing differences
affinity-estimation | /əˈfɪnəti ˌestɪˈmeɪʃn/ | n | quickly sorting many items into size groups by similarity
cone-of-uncertainty | /koʊn əv ʌnˈsɜːrtnti/ | n | the idea that estimates get more accurate as the work proceeds
capacity-planning | /kəˈpæsəti ˈplænɪŋ/ | n | working out how much a team can realistically take on
ideal-time | /aɪˈdiːəl taɪm/ | n | how long work would take with no interruptions at all
monte-carlo-forecasting | /ˌmɑːnti ˈkɑːrloʊ ˈfɔːrkæstɪŋ/ | n | forecasting outcomes by running many randomised simulations
wideband-delphi | /ˈwaɪdbænd ˈdelfaɪ/ | n | repeated anonymous estimating rounds until experts converge
t-shirt-sizing | /ˈtiː ʃɜːrt ˈsaɪzɪŋ/ | n | sizing work roughly as S, M, L or XL instead of in numbers

## event
retrospective | /ˌretrəˈspektɪv/ | n | a regular meeting where the team inspects and improves how it works
daily-standup | /ˈdeɪli ˈstændʌp/ | n | a short daily meeting to sync on progress and surface blockers
sprint-review | /sprɪnt rɪˈvjuː/ | n | a session where the team shows the increment and gathers feedback
sprint-planning | /sprɪnt ˈplænɪŋ/ | n | the meeting where the team decides what to build this sprint and how
scrum-of-scrums | /skrʌm əv skrʌmz/ | n | a coordination meeting between representatives of several teams
sprint-demo | /sprɪnt ˈdemoʊ/ | n | showing working software to stakeholders at the end of a cycle
sprint-goal | /sprɪnt ɡoʊl/ | n | the single objective the sprint is meant to achieve

## exam-phrase
phrase-which-of-the-following | /wɪtʃ əv ðə ˈfɑːloʊɪŋ/ | phr | introduces a list from which you must pick one
phrase-best-way | /ðə best weɪ tuː/ | phr | asks for the single most effective option, not merely a correct one
phrase-most-likely | /moʊst ˈlaɪkli/ | phr | asks which outcome or action has the highest probability
phrase-in-order-to | /ɪn ˈɔːrdər tuː/ | phr | signals the purpose or goal of an action
phrase-as-a-result | /əz ə rɪˈzʌlt əv/ | phr | signals a consequence caused by what came before
phrase-due-to | /duː tuː/ | phr | signals the cause or reason for something
phrase-prior-to | /ˈpraɪər tuː/ | phr | before something else happens; a formal way of saying "before"
phrase-with-respect-to | /wɪð rɪˈspekt tuː/ | phr | concerning, or in relation to, a particular subject
phrase-in-the-context-of | /ɪn ðə ˈkɑːntekst əv/ | phr | within the specific situation being described
phrase-on-behalf-of | /ɑːn bɪˈhæf əv/ | phr | acting for someone else or representing their interests
phrase-in-accordance-with | /ɪn əˈkɔːrdns wɪð/ | phr | following a rule, standard or agreement exactly
phrase-at-this-point | /ət ðɪs pɔɪnt/ | phr | at this particular stage of the situation
phrase-going-forward | /ˈɡoʊɪŋ ˈfɔːrwərd/ | phr | from now on, in the future
phrase-responsible-for | /ɪz rɪˈspɑːnsəbl fɔːr/ | phr | has the duty to do or take care of something
phrase-regardless-of | /rɪˈɡɑːrdləs əv/ | phr | without being affected by; no matter what
phrase-in-light-of | /ɪn laɪt əv/ | phr | taking new information into account
phrase-given-that | /ˈɡɪvn ðæt/ | phr | since this fact is true; introduces a premise you must accept
phrase-so-that | /soʊ ðæt/ | phr | introduces the intended result of an action
phrase-rather-than | /ˈræðər ðæn/ | phr | instead of; marks a deliberate choice between two options
phrase-instead-of | /ɪnˈsted əv/ | phr | in place of something else

## facilitation
fist-of-five | /fɪst əv faɪv/ | n | voting with zero to five fingers to show level of agreement
dot-voting | /dɑːt ˈvoʊtɪŋ/ | n | prioritising by letting people place a limited number of dots on options
conflict-resolution-modes | /ˈkɑːnflɪkt ˌrezəˈluːʃn moʊdz/ | n | the five recognised styles for handling conflict
brainstorming | /ˈbreɪnstɔːrmɪŋ/ | n | generating many ideas quickly without judging them
facilitation | /fəˌsɪlɪˈteɪʃn/ | n | guiding a group's process so it reaches its own decisions
affinity-diagram | /əˈfɪnəti ˈdaɪəɡræm/ | n | grouping many ideas into natural clusters
parking-lot | /ˈpɑːrkɪŋ lɑːt/ | n | a visible list of off-topic issues saved for later

## finance
roi | /rɪˈtɜːrn ɑːn ɪnˈvestmənt/ | n | the gain from an investment relative to its cost
npv | /net ˈprezənt ˈvæljuː/ | n | today's value of future cash flows minus the investment
cost-of-delay | /kɔːst əv dɪˈleɪ/ | n | the money lost for every unit of time a feature is late
fixed-price-contract | /fɪkst praɪs/ | n | an agreement where the price is set in advance and does not change

## flow
wip-limit | /ˈwɪp ˈlɪmɪt/ | n | a cap on how many items may be in progress at once
little-law | /ˈlɪtlz lɔː/ | n | the rule that cycle time equals work in progress divided by throughput
flow-efficiency | /floʊ ɪˈfɪʃnsi/ | n | the share of total elapsed time in which work was actually being done
pull-system | /pʊl ˈsɪstəm/ | n | new work is started only when capacity frees up

## framework
kanban | /ˈkɑːnbɑːn/ | n | a method for visualising work, limiting WIP and improving flow
xp | /ɪkˈstriːm ˈproʊɡræmɪŋ/ | n | an agile method built on tight engineering practices and fast feedback
less | /les/ | n | a framework for scaling Scrum to many teams on one product
hybrid-approach | /ˈhaɪbrɪd əˈproʊtʃ/ | n | deliberately mixing predictive and adaptive ways of working
scrumban | /ˈskrʌmbæn/ | n | a blend of Scrum's structure with Kanban's flow control
scrum | /skrʌm/ | n | a lightweight framework of fixed roles, events and artifacts
safe | /skeɪld ˈædʒaɪl ˈfreɪmwɜːrk/ | n | a framework for coordinating agile work at enterprise scale
dsdm | /ˌdiː es diː ˈem/ | n | an agile method that fixes time and cost and varies scope
feature-driven-development | /ˈfiːtʃər ˌdrɪvn dɪˈveləpmənt/ | n | an agile method organised around a list of small client-valued features
disciplined-agile | /ˈdɪsəplɪnd ˈædʒaɪl/ | n | a toolkit that helps a team choose its own way of working

## leadership
servant-leadership | /ˈsɜːrvənt ˈliːdərʃɪp/ | n | leading by serving the team's needs and removing its obstacles
situational-leadership | /ˌsɪtʃuˈeɪʃənl ˈliːdərʃɪp/ | n | adjusting your leadership style to each person's readiness
emotional-intelligence | /ɪˈmoʊʃənl ɪnˈtelɪdʒəns/ | n | recognising and managing emotions, in yourself and in others
adaptive-leadership | /əˈdæptɪv ˈliːdərʃɪp/ | n | leading change by helping people adapt rather than by giving answers
coaching | /ˈkoʊtʃɪŋ/ | n | helping someone find their own answers through questions
mentoring | /ˈmentərɪŋ/ | n | guiding someone less experienced by sharing your own experience

## metric
velocity | /vəˈlɑːsəti/ | n | how many story points a team completes per iteration
burndown-chart | /ˈbɜːrndaʊn tʃɑːrt/ | n | a chart showing remaining work falling towards zero over time
burnup-chart | /ˈbɜːrnʌp tʃɑːrt/ | n | a chart showing completed work rising towards a (possibly moving) scope line
cumulative-flow | /ˈkjuːmjələtɪv floʊ ˈdaɪəɡræm/ | n | a stacked area chart revealing bottlenecks and growing queues
cycle-time | /ˈsaɪkl taɪm/ | n | how long an item takes from the moment work starts to when it is done
lead-time | /ˈliːd taɪm/ | n | how long an item takes from request to delivery
throughput | /ˈθruːpʊt/ | n | how many items are finished per unit of time
evm | /ɜːrnd ˈvæljuː ˈmænɪdʒmənt/ | n | measuring progress by comparing planned, earned and actual value
cpi | /kɔːst pərˈfɔːrməns ˈɪndeks/ | n | earned value divided by actual cost; above 1 means under budget
spi | /ˈskedʒuːl pərˈfɔːrməns ˈɪndeks/ | n | earned value divided by planned value; above 1 means ahead of schedule
net-promoter-score | /net prəˈmoʊtər skɔːr/ | n | a loyalty measure based on how likely customers are to recommend you
variance-analysis | /ˈveriəns əˈnæləsɪs/ | n | examining the gap between what was planned and what happened

## mindset
agile | /ˈædʒaɪl/ | adj | able to move and change direction quickly and easily
kaizen | /ˈkaɪzen/ | n | continuous improvement through many small changes
sustainable-pace | /səˈsteɪnəbl peɪs/ | n | a workload the team can keep up indefinitely without burning out
real-options | /ˈriːəl ˈɑːpʃnz/ | n | treating decisions as options that are worth keeping open until they expire
last-responsible-moment | /læst rɪˈspɑːnsəbl ˈmoʊmənt/ | n | the latest point at which a decision can still be made without harm
psychological-safety | /ˌsaɪkəˈlɑːdʒɪkl ˈseɪfti/ | n | a shared belief that speaking up will not be punished
shu-ha-ri | /ˌʃuː hɑː ˈriː/ | n | the three stages of mastery: follow, adapt, then transcend the rules
agile-manifesto | /ˈædʒaɪl ˌmænɪˈfestoʊ/ | n | the 2001 statement of four agile values and twelve principles
continuous-improvement | /kənˈtɪnjuəs ɪmˈpruːvmənt/ | n | steadily making small betterments rather than waiting for a big fix
systems-thinking | /ˈsɪstəmz ˈθɪŋkɪŋ/ | n | looking at how parts interact rather than optimising one part alone
cynefin-framework | /ˈkʌnɪvɪn/ | n | a sense-making model sorting situations as clear, complicated, complex or chaotic
muda | /ˈmuːdə/ | n | waste; any activity that consumes resources without adding value
lean | /liːn/ | n | maximising value while systematically eliminating waste
transparency | /trænsˈpærənsi/ | n | making the real state of the work visible to everyone
inspection | /ɪnˈspekʃn/ | n | frequently examining artifacts and progress to detect problems early
adaptation | /ˌædæpˈteɪʃn/ | n | adjusting the process or product as soon as inspection reveals a problem
empirical-process-control | /ɪmˈpɪrɪkl ˈprɑːses kənˈtroʊl/ | n | steering by observation and evidence rather than by up-front prediction
agile-principles | /ˈædʒaɪl ˈprɪnsəplz/ | n | the twelve statements that expand on the agile values

## people
stakeholder | /ˈsteɪkhoʊldər/ | n | anyone who affects, or is affected by, the project

## practice
backlog-refinement | /ˈbæklɑːɡ rɪˈfaɪnmənt/ | n | ongoing work to clarify, split and re-order backlog items
timebox | /ˈtaɪmbɑːks/ | n | a fixed maximum period allotted to an activity
spike | /spaɪk/ | n | a short time-boxed investigation to answer a technical or design question
rolling-wave-planning | /ˈroʊlɪŋ weɪv ˈplænɪŋ/ | n | planning near-term work in detail and later work only roughly
agile-contract | /ˈædʒaɪl ˈkɑːntrækt/ | n | an agreement designed to allow scope to change as learning happens
value-stream-mapping | /ˈvæljuː striːm ˈmæpɪŋ/ | n | drawing every step from request to delivery to expose waste
progressive-elaboration | /prəˈɡresɪv ɪˌlæbəˈreɪʃn/ | n | adding detail to plans gradually as more becomes known
lessons-learned | /ˈlesnz lɜːrnd/ | n | knowledge captured from experience so it can be reused
story-mapping | /ˈstɔːri ˈmæpɪŋ/ | n | arranging stories along a user's journey to see scope and slice releases
swarming | /ˈswɔːrmɪŋ/ | n | the whole team converging on one item to finish it fast
adaptive-planning | /əˈdæptɪv ˈplænɪŋ/ | n | replanning continuously as feedback arrives
feedback-loop | /ˈfiːdbæk luːp/ | n | a cycle in which output is observed and used to adjust the next input
iterative-development | /ˈɪtərətɪv dɪˈveləpmənt/ | n | building in repeated cycles, refining the product each time

## prioritization
moscow | /ˈmɑːskaʊ/ | n | sorting requirements into Must, Should, Could and Won't have
kano | /ˈkɑːnoʊ ˈmɑːdl/ | n | a model classifying features by how they affect customer satisfaction
wsjf | /ˈweɪtɪd ˈʃɔːrtɪst dʒɑːb fɜːrst/ | n | prioritising by cost of delay divided by job size

## product
mvp | /ˈmɪnɪməm ˈvaɪəbl ˈprɑːdʌkt/ | n | the smallest version that delivers value and produces learning
mmf | /ˈmɪnɪməm ˈmɑːrkɪtəbl ˈfiːtʃər/ | n | the smallest feature chunk that customers would actually pay for
product-vision | /ˈprɑːdʌkt ˈvɪʒn/ | n | a short statement of what the product is for and for whom
roadmap | /ˈroʊdmæp/ | n | a high-level view of what is intended over the coming months
persona | /pərˈsoʊnə/ | n | a fictional but research-based profile of a typical user
prototype | /ˈproʊtətaɪp/ | n | an early model built to test an idea before building it properly

## quality
acceptance-criteria | /əkˈseptəns kraɪˈtɪriən/ | n | the conditions a story must meet to be accepted
definition-of-done | /ˌdefɪˈnɪʃn əv dʌn/ | n | the shared checklist every increment must satisfy before it counts as finished
definition-of-ready | /ˌdefɪˈnɪʃn əv ˈredi/ | n | the agreed conditions an item must meet before the team will start it
escaped-defect | /ɪˈskeɪpt ˈdiːfekt/ | n | a bug that reached production without being caught
acceptance-test | /əkˈseptəns test/ | n | a test proving the feature meets the customer's stated need
exploratory-testing | /ɪkˈsplɔːrətɔːri ˈtestɪŋ/ | n | testing where design and execution happen together, guided by what you find

## requirement
user-story | /ˈjuːzər ˈstɔːri/ | n | a short description of a need from the user's point of view
non-functional-requirement | /nɑːn ˈfʌŋkʃənl rɪˈkwaɪərmənt/ | n | a quality constraint such as speed, security or usability
invest-criteria | /ɪnˈvest/ | n | six qualities of a good story: independent, negotiable, valuable, estimable, small, testable

## risk
risk-adjusted-backlog | /rɪsk əˈdʒʌstɪd ˈbæklɑːɡ/ | n | a backlog that also contains items for reducing risk
fishbone-diagram | /ˈfɪʃboʊn ˈdaɪəɡræm/ | n | a diagram that sorts possible causes of a problem into categories
five-whys | /faɪv waɪz/ | n | asking "why" repeatedly until the underlying cause appears
root-cause-analysis | /ruːt kɔːz əˈnæləsɪs/ | n | finding the real origin of a problem rather than treating its symptoms
premortem | /ˌpriːˈmɔːrtəm/ | n | imagining the project has already failed, then listing why
risk-register | /rɪsk ˈredʒɪstər/ | n | a living list of risks with their likelihood, impact and response
risk-burndown-chart | /rɪsk ˈbɜːrndaʊn tʃɑːrt/ | n | a chart tracking total risk exposure falling over time
escalation | /ˌeskəˈleɪʃn/ | n | raising an issue to a higher level of authority for a decision

## role
agile-coach | /ˈædʒaɪl koʊtʃ/ | n | someone who helps teams and organisations improve how they work
scrum-master | /skrʌm ˈmæstər/ | n | the person accountable for the team's effectiveness and for removing impediments
product-owner | /ˈprɑːdʌkt ˈoʊnər/ | n | the person accountable for maximising the product's value
release-train-engineer | /rɪˈliːs treɪn ˌendʒɪˈnɪr/ | n | the servant leader who facilitates delivery across many teams

## team
agile-team | /ˈædʒaɪl tiːm/ | n | a small, cross-functional group that delivers value together
working-agreement | /ˈwɜːrkɪŋ əˈɡriːmənt/ | n | the team's own written rules for how it will work together
team-charter | /tiːm ˈtʃɑːrtər/ | n | a document capturing the team's purpose, values and ground rules
cross-functional | /krɔːs ˈfʌŋkʃənl/ | adj | having all the skills needed to finish the work inside one team
self-organizing | /self ˈɔːrɡənaɪzɪŋ/ | adj | deciding for itself how to do the work, without being told
t-shaped-skills | /ˈtiː ʃeɪpt skɪlz/ | n | deep expertise in one area plus useful breadth across others
tuckman-stages | /ˈtʌkmən/ | n | the forming, storming, norming, performing model of team development
co-location | /ˌkoʊ loʊˈkeɪʃn/ | n | having the whole team physically together in one space
distributed-team | /dɪˈstrɪbjuːtɪd tiːm/ | n | a team whose members work from different locations
scrum-team | /skrʌm tiːm/ | n | the product owner, scrum master and developers together
whole-team | /hoʊl tiːm/ | n | the principle that quality and delivery are everyone's responsibility

## value
value-stream | /ˈvæljuː striːm/ | n | every step taken to turn a request into delivered value

## vocabulary
vocab-practitioner | /prækˈtɪʃənər/ | n | someone who actively does a job or applies a discipline
vocab-scenario | /səˈnærioʊ/ | n | a described situation, real or imagined
vocab-sponsor | /ˈspɑːnsər/ | n | the person who funds and champions the project
vocab-bottleneck | /ˈbɑːtlnek/ | n | the point where flow is most restricted and work piles up
vocab-deliverable | /dɪˈlɪvərəbl/ | n | any tangible output the project must produce
vocab-ensure | /ɪnˈʃʊr/ | v | to make certain that something happens
vocab-documentation | /ˌdɑːkjumenˈteɪʃn/ | n | written material that records or explains something
vocab-constraint | /kənˈstreɪnt/ | n | a limit you must work within
vocab-flexibility | /ˌfleksəˈbɪləti/ | n | the ability to change easily when circumstances change
vocab-compliance | /kəmˈplaɪəns/ | n | acting in line with rules, laws or standards
